#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8535759b-fe73-52af-853f-f3486f41d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8535759b-fe73-52af-853f-f3486f41d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ab610c-28ab-5d09-83f7-d68b274b353d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89927db-d31d-51e0-b063-5d1318a6634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89927db-d31d-51e0-b063-5d1318a6634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa355cf-d45f-5b54-bcec-6c9a1e489b0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29e3e5d7-28ea-5e8a-b874-caa92a5ebba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336597" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{-4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4t}\\cdot (-2{e}^{2t})-0\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ab3da8-8b65-5c7c-b413-f64ac221623d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531178} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dd0ba3-3b82-5d28-920c-a872d1b66617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531213} {:answer/id #uuid "a66e5a36-33aa-57dd-9127-3bf14e2a3526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531223} {:answer/id #uuid "bc060e7c-ade5-5987-bfcc-22a3b89b7c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91ab3da8-8b65-5c7c-b413-f64ac221623d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531178} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d19aee-5b0e-5d1e-a94c-b57fbd271c20" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06460bbd-b07c-5c78-a2a2-80d558e6fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530872} {:answer/id #uuid "0880df10-3963-5d13-be2c-f4f7b1243830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "line $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530880} {:answer/id #uuid "1e95d564-42f5-5e8c-87b1-05cb232304bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "solution of the system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530921} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1e7de1-308b-5df0-8d07-32baa3c11686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "first shifting theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06460bbd-b07c-5c78-a2a2-80d558e6fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530872} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfeef9c0-222d-580e-9176-2a58212a74b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531536} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc6ec5e-e5a6-5494-ba80-6ac0d18a888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531029} {:answer/id #uuid "50c6f9fd-4090-51e9-b85f-8edff140d4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531039} {:answer/id #uuid "82bcbc16-1b95-5f7c-ade5-752010bacc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7bc21d-2613-5254-8ca6-da3ab5ba690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc7bc21d-2613-5254-8ca6-da3ab5ba690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531317} :answer-field/id #uuid "e749236f-06ab-5ec0-b55c-1658240128bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59d9272b-7eb2-549a-868b-86d14c7fdef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336703" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6418cc68-5c14-5b79-a76b-bd6a187118df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22t-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6418cc68-5c14-5b79-a76b-bd6a187118df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22t-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802} :answer-field/id #uuid "108a4f0f-4662-57f5-8305-95c9666f98fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa173e6f-437b-589d-80f8-b753b282ebf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337219" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})\\cdot (1\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t{e}^{-2t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12(t-1)-2(2t+1) \\\\ 24(t-1)-(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8t-14 \\\\ 22t-25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d66787-66e5-569a-91da-fb16e858ec7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d66787-66e5-569a-91da-fb16e858ec7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309980} :answer-field/id #uuid "33380138-39c7-5cc1-be02-40c0352385d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57fa1d40-985b-5dc8-bff3-11325100380c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fa1d40-985b-5dc8-bff3-11325100380c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411} :answer-field/id #uuid "74228cec-035c-567c-bcee-7a90efcf66d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c98926c-5044-5e94-be2a-fc638fdc3f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-337230" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -8{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca5fb1c3-f4d9-5a37-a9bd-6708fe402752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca5fb1c3-f4d9-5a37-a9bd-6708fe402752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365810} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af25afe-e912-5735-b096-591cb5639115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9269cd51-19e6-5e98-96ab-0e051bb84772" :question/math-academy-id "q-337290" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | $-2t-3$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-(-{e}^{t})\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{4}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{8}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{8}{3}t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{8}{3}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1)-8(t+1) \\\\ 2(2t-1)+8(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2t-3 \\\\ 4t+2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
